A Day in the Life of a Student

Mark wakes up at seven o'clock. He does not want to get up. His bed is warm, but the room is cold. He looks at his phone. It says 7:05. He sighs and sits up.

He goes to the bathroom. He washes his face and brushes his teeth. The water is cold. He looks in the mirror and thinks, "I need a haircut."

He goes to the kitchen. His mother is already there. She is making coffee. She says, "Good morning, Mark. Did you sleep well?" He says, "Not really. I went to bed too late."

He eats breakfast. He has bread with butter and cheese. He drinks a cup of coffee. His mother asks, "What do you have at school today?" He says, "Math, history, and English. Nothing special."

He puts on his clothes. He wears a blue shirt and black jeans. He takes his backpack. He says goodbye to his mother and leaves the house.

Outside, it is raining. Mark does not have an umbrella. He puts his hood on and walks quickly. The street is wet and gray. Cars are driving slowly. People are walking with umbrellas.

He arrives at the bus stop. There are five other people there. An old woman is holding a small dog. A young man is listening to music. Mark waits for the bus.

The bus comes at 7:40. It is full of people. Mark stands near the door. He holds the handrail. The bus moves slowly through the traffic.

At school, Mark goes to his first class. It is math. The teacher writes numbers on the board. Mark does not understand. He asks his friend, "Do you understand this?" His friend says, "No, not at all."

The second class is history. The teacher talks about ancient Rome. Mark likes history. He takes notes in his notebook. He writes three pages.

At lunch, Mark sits with his friends. They eat sandwiches and talk about football. His friend Alex says, "Did you see the game last night?" Mark says, "No, I was studying." Alex says, "You missed a great game."

After lunch, Mark has English class. The teacher asks him to read a text. He reads slowly and makes two mistakes. The teacher says, "Good job, Mark. Keep practicing."

At three o'clock, school ends. Mark walks home. The rain has stopped. The sun is coming out. He feels tired but happy.

At home, he eats a snack. He drinks tea and eats a cookie. His mother asks, "How was school?" He says, "It was okay. I am tired."

He goes to his room. He does his homework. He writes a short essay about Rome. It takes him one hour. He checks his phone. He has three messages from Alex.

He answers the messages. Then he lies on his bed. He thinks about the day. He thinks about the math test next week. He feels a little worried.

In the evening, he eats dinner with his family. They have soup and bread. His father talks about his work. His mother talks about the garden. Mark listens and eats.

After dinner, he watches a movie with his sister. It is a comedy. They laugh a lot. His sister says, "This is a good movie." He says, "Yes, I like it too."

